DIRENT(3)                      P32W Unix                       DIRENT(3)

NAME
       dirent, opendir, readdir, closedir - read directories

SYNOPSIS
       #include <dirent.h>

       DIR *opendir(const char *path);
       struct dirent *readdir(DIR *d);
       int closedir(DIR *d);

       struct dirent {
           unsigned d_ino;     /* inode number */
           char d_name[29];    /* name, NUL-ended */
       };

DESCRIPTION
       A directory is a file of 32-byte entries: a 4-byte inode
       number and a name of up to 28 characters (fs(5)). These
       functions read it.

       opendir opens the directory path and returns a DIR for it
       (allocated by malloc(3); its fd member is the descriptor).

       readdir returns the next entry of d, in the order they lie
       in the directory, . and .. included; empty slots (inode 0),
       left by removed files, are skipped.

       closedir closes the descriptor and frees d.

RETURN VALUE
       opendir returns the DIR, or NULL with errno set. readdir
       returns a pointer to a struct dirent inside d, overwritten by
       the next readdir, or NULL at the end of the directory (or on
       a read error). closedir returns 0, or -1 with errno set.

ERRORS
       opendir fails with the errors of open(2) (ENOENT, EACCES,
       ...), with ENOTDIR if path is not a directory, and with
       ENOMEM.

EXAMPLES
       DIR *d = opendir(".");
       struct dirent *e;
       if (d == NULL) { perror("."); exit(1); }
       while ((e = readdir(d)) != NULL)
           if (e->d_name[0] != '.')
               printf("%s\n", e->d_name);
       closedir(d);

SEE ALSO
       ls(1), open(2), stat(2), getcwd(3), fs(5)

BUGS
       The entries are not sorted. There is no rewinddir, seekdir,
       telldir or dirfd, and no d_type: use stat(2) to tell a
       directory from a file. Reading needs read permission on the
       directory.
